---
import BlogArticle from '../../components/BlogArticle.astro';
import BlogCode from '../../components/BlogCode.astro';

const contents = [
  { id: 'marking', label: 'An agent that marks its own homework' },
  { id: 'options', label: 'Three places an agent can test' },
  { id: 'loop', label: 'What a real error does for an agent' },
  { id: 'try-it', label: 'Connect an agent' },
];
const agentConnect = `localcloud doctor
localcloud mcp install --client claude-code`;
const agentRule = `Fetch https://local.cloud/ai/agents.md and start or reuse LocalCloud.
Use only returned localhost endpoints and AnonymousCredentials().
Stop if an operation is unavailable locally; never request real GCP credentials.`;
---

<BlogArticle
  slug="why-ai-agents-need-local-cloud"
  kind="Perspective"
  deck="Ask a coding agent for a BigQuery loader and it will write the loader, the test and the mock the test runs against. All three agree with each other. None of them has met BigQuery. Agents need something real to be wrong against."
  primary={{ href: '/docs/mcp/', label: 'MCP setup guide' }}
  secondary={{ href: '/local-cloud-for-ai-agents/', label: 'Local cloud for AI agents' }}
  {contents}
  next={{ posts: ['localcloud-mcp-agent-cloud', 'parallel-git-worktrees-localcloud'], doc: { href: '/docs/mcp/', label: 'MCP guide: every supported coding client' } }}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="marking">An agent that marks its own homework</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">A human who writes a mock usually knows what the real service does and encodes it. An agent that writes a mock encodes what it believes the service does, which is the same belief that produced the code under test. If it thinks a method takes a string, the mock accepts a string, the test passes, and the pull request arrives green.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">The first real check happens after merge, in an environment where failures are expensive. Query syntax that doesn't parse, a topic that was never created, an endpoint option that doesn't exist: all invisible until then.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="options">Three places an agent can test</h2>
    <table class="blog-compare mt-5">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Mocks</th><th scope="col">A real project</th><th scope="col">LocalCloud</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Does the service push back?</th><td>No, the agent wrote both sides</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes, through the real SDK</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Credentials in the agent's hands</th><td>None</td><td>A key that works on your cloud</td><td>None</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Cost of a retry loop at 3 a.m.</th><td>Nothing</td><td>Your bill</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Safe to delete everything?</th><td>Nothing to delete</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, and reset in a second</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="mt-4">The middle column is the honest one and the dangerous one. An autonomous process holding a service-account key, free to create and delete, is a risk most teams rightly refuse. The right-hand column keeps the honesty and drops the key: every client talks to <code>127.0.0.1</code> with anonymous credentials, and no production project ID ever enters the agent's context.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="loop">What a real error does for an agent</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Agents are good at fixing errors they can read. Give one a genuine exception from the client library, such as a missing topic or a schema mismatch, and it will usually find the cause and correct it without being asked. Give it a passing mock and it has nothing to work with.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">LocalCloud adds an MCP server so the agent doesn't have to guess at the environment either. It can ask which services are running, whether an operation is supported, and what endpoints to use, then run your integration tests and read the results. The loop of write, run, read the failure and fix happens before you are asked to review anything.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">The <a href="/blog/localcloud-mcp-agent-cloud/">MCP walkthrough</a> shows that loop end to end, including an agent finding and fixing a bytes-versus-text bug that a mock would have hidden.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Connect an agent</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">With the CLI installed from the <a href="/docs/#install-the-cli">quick start</a>, register the MCP server with your client. Claude Code is shown; the <a href="/docs/mcp/#choose-your-coding-client">client table</a> covers Cursor, Codex, VS Code, Windsurf, Cline, Gemini CLI and others.</p>
    <BlogCode label="Check Docker and register the MCP server" code={agentConnect} copy="copy-blog-ai-mocks-install" />
    <p class="mt-4">Then tell the agent where the boundary is. Put this in your repository's <code>AGENTS.md</code>:</p>
    <BlogCode label="Rule for AGENTS.md" code={agentRule} copy="copy-blog-ai-mocks-prompt" wrap />
    <p class="mt-4">The last line of that rule is the important one. An agent that stops when something isn't available locally is one you can leave running. Review its pull request as you would anyone's, and let your staging checks have the final word.</p>
  </section>
</BlogArticle>
